    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable that this outcome raises questions for you. When there is a noticeable disparity in traditional academic metrics (completed PhD vs. ABD, six publications vs. two, four years of university teaching) combined with an entirely male panel and silence to a feedback request, suspicion of conscious or unconscious bias is a natural reaction.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what an all-male panel signifies, how academic hiring committees typically operate, and how you might strategically handle this situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is an all-male panel a \"sign\" of anything?\n\n#### A. Institutional Practice and Governance\n* **Policy compliance:** At many public universities today, HR policies explicitly mandate diversity on hiring committees (e.g., requiring at least one woman or an external diversity representative). An all-male panel may indicate:\n  * A department that failed to comply with institutional Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion (EDI) recruitment guidelines.\n  * An institution whose policies allow exemptions if the department has few senior female faculty.\n* **Economics demographics:** Economics notoriously suffers from severe underrepresentation of women at senior ranks. While demographic reality may explain *why* four male faculty were on the committee, best practices usually require bringing in an external faculty member to avoid an all-male panel.\n\n#### B. Risk of Unconscious Bias\nDecades of research in sociology and behavioral economics demonstrate that homogeneous panels are more vulnerable to **in-group favoritism**, affinity bias, and shifting evaluation criteria (e.g., weighing \"industry experience\" more heavily when evaluating a male candidate while strictly scrutinizing traditional academic criteria for others). \n\nHowever, **an all-male panel alone is not legal proof of sex discrimination.** It is circumstantial context that warrants closer scrutiny, not a standalone verdict.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Other Factors Often at Play in Academic Hiring\n\nWhile your academic profile appears stronger on standard metrics, hiring committees often weigh factors that are not visible in the job announcement:\n\n* **Curriculum and Accreditation Fit:** If the economics department is housed within or affiliated with a business school (AACSB, EQUIS, or AMBA accredited), \"practical\" or \"industry\" experience is often heavily incentivized to meet accreditation standards for applied courses (e.g., finance, consulting, applied forecasting).\n* **The Interview and Job Talk / Teaching Demo:** In lecturer positions, committees frequently place substantial weight on the live teaching demonstration, student interaction, and how well the candidate fits the specific course schedule needing coverage.\n* **Sub-field alignment:** A candidate whose specific niche aligns with an urgent curriculum gap (e.g., financial econometrics or machine learning) may be favored over a candidate with more publications in an area the department already covers well.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Silence\n\nTwo weeks without a response to a request for written feedback is frustrating, but it is standard academic bureaucracy:\n* **HR processes:** Formal feedback often requires HR clearance or committee sign-off to ensure statements do not expose the university to legal liability.\n* **Administrative delay:** Committee chairs often prioritize ongoing teaching and research over debriefing unsuccessful candidates.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\nBefore deciding whether to escalate or make a formal issue of the panel’s composition, consider the following pragmatic steps:\n\n#### Step 1: Check Institutional Recruitment Policies\nLook up the university’s public HR policies or staff handbooks regarding **recruitment and selection panels**:\n* Does the university have an explicit rule requiring gender-balanced hiring panels?\n* Does it require panel members to have documented unconscious bias training?\nIf the panel violated the university's own stated hiring protocols, you have a much stronger basis for an administrative inquiry or procedural appeal.\n\n#### Step 2: Send a Professional Follow-Up for Feedback\nSend a polite, forward-looking follow-up email to the committee chair or HR contact. Frame it around career development rather than grievance:\n> *\"Dear [Chair/HR], I am following up on my request from two weeks ago regarding feedback on my interview for the Lecturer in Economics position. As I continue to apply for academic roles, constructive feedback on how I can strengthen future applications would be immensely valuable. I would appreciate any notes or guidance the panel can share.\"*\n\n#### Step 3: Understand Your Rights to Documentation\nAt public universities (depending on your jurisdiction, e.g., the UK, EU, Australia, or various US states):\n* You may have legal rights under **Freedom of Information (FOI)** or **Subject Access Requests (SAR / GDPR)** to request the rubric scores, scoring matrices, and interview notes related to your application.\n* These notes will reveal whether the criteria shifted or whether objective scoring guidelines were followed.\n\n#### Step 4: Consult Discreetly\nBefore lodging a formal complaint or grievance:\n* **Academic union or staff association:** If you belong to a union (such as UCU, NTEU, AAUP, etc.), speak to a caseworker. They have seen similar cases and can tell you if this specific institution has a history of procedural irregularities.\n* **Senior mentors:** Consult a trusted senior mentor in your field outside that university to get an objective read on the department’s reputation and advice on navigating academic politics.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nAn all-male panel in an economics hire is a legitimate red flag regarding the department's recruitment governance and attention to EDI standards. However, turning this into a formal challenge requires verifiable evidence—starting with whether internal recruitment rules were broken and what the written scoring records show. Focus first on obtaining your feedback and examining institutional policy before deciding how far to pursue the matter.\n",
